{"name":"Open Sesame","hex":"#f8e2a9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"open sesame","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Phrase","definitions":["open up"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any successful means of achieving a result, especially means that are magical or technical, or otherwise beyond the understanding of most people."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/open_sesame"}],"entries":[{"title":"Open sesame","blocks":[{"text":"\"Open sesame\" is a magical phrase in the story of \"Ali Baba and the Forty Thieves\" in Antoine Galland's version of One Thousand and One Nights. It opens the mouth of a cave in which forty thieves have hidden a treasure."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/%D7%A2%D7%9C%D7%99_%D7%91%D7%90%D7%91%D7%90_%D7%9E%D7%AA%D7%97%D7%91%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%94%D7%A2%D7%A5.jpg/330px-%D7%A2%D7%9C%D7%99_%D7%91%D7%90%D7%91%D7%90_%D7%9E%D7%AA%D7%97%D7%91%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%94%D7%A2%D7%A5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_sesame","wikidata":"Q2222713"},{"title":"Open Sesame (Leila K song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Open Sesame\" is a song by Swedish Eurodance artist Leila K, released in October 1992 by Coma label as the lead single from the artist's second album, Carousel (1993). Co-written and co-produced by Denniz PoP, the song was a huge success in many European countries in 1993. It reached number-one in Belgium and peaked within the top 10 in at least nine other countries. The accompanying music video was directed by Matt Broadley and filmed in London, depicting Leila K as the Queen of Sheba. In 1999, the song was released in a new remix as \"Open Sesame '99\". Dutch singer Daisy Dee released a cover in 2000, which reached number 78 in Germany."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/f0/Leila_-_OpenSesame_single.jpg/330px-Leila_-_OpenSesame_single.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_Sesame_(Leila_K_song)","wikidata":"Q7096195"},{"title":"Sesame","blocks":[{"text":"Sesame is a plant in the genus Sesamum, also called benne. Numerous wild relatives occur in Africa and a smaller number in India. It is widely naturalized in tropical regions around the world and is cultivated for its edible seeds, which grow in pods. World production in 2024 was 6.7 million tonnes, with India, Myanmar, and Sudan as the largest producers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Sesamum_indicum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-129.jpg/330px-Sesamum_indicum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-129.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sesame","wikidata":"Q2763698"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Sesamum_indicum_2.jpg/120px-Sesamum_indicum_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Sesamum_indicum_2.jpg/250px-Sesamum_indicum_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Sesamum_indicum_2.jpg/120px-Sesamum_indicum_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASesamum_indicum_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Sasame_Plant%2C_Behbahan.jpg/250px-Sasame_Plant%2C_Behbahan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Sasame_Plant%2C_Behbahan.jpg/330px-Sasame_Plant%2C_Behbahan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Sasame_Plant%2C_Behbahan.jpg/